A Schema defines document shape, types, validation, defaults, and indexes; a Model provides collection operations. Example: `const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);`.

```javascript
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: { type: String, required: true, trim: true },
  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true }
}, { timestamps: true });
const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);
```

A JWT has header, payload, and signature. Claims such as `sub`, `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, and `iat` describe identity and validity; the payload is encoded, not encrypted, so never put secrets in it.

```javascript
