}
```

A ruleset has a selector followed by a declaration block. Each declaration is a property and value separated by a colon and normally terminated by a semicolon. Comments use /* ... */. Invalid declarations are generally ignored, so inspect braces, colons, semicolons, spelling and valid units when debugging.

```css
.card {
  padding: 1rem;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
}
```

Selectors identify the elements a rule styles. Universal (*) matches all elements; type selectors match tags; .class and #id target class and ID attributes; selector lists share declarations; compound selectors require multiple conditions on one element. Prefer reusable classes over IDs for styling.

```css
.card { padding: 1rem; }
#main-title { color: navy; }
input[type="email"] { width: 100%; }
```

Combinators express relationships: a b selects any b descendant of a; a > b selects direct children; a + b selects the immediately following sibling; a ~ b selects later siblings sharing the same parent. Use them to express structure without adding unnecessary classes.

```css
.menu > li { list-style: none; }
.card h2 { margin-block: 0; }
h2 + p { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
```

Attribute selectors target markup attributes: [disabled] checks presence, [type="email"] checks an exact value, [href^="https"] checks a prefix, [href$=".pdf"] a suffix, [class~="active"] a whitespace-separated token, and [data-x*="item"] a substring. They are useful for forms and data-driven states.

```css
a[href^="https"] { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }
input[required] { border-color: crimson; }
```

@layer establishes named groups of rules with an explicit precedence order. For normal declarations, later layers outrank earlier layers, while unlayered normal declarations outrank layered normal declarations. Important declarations reverse layer order. Declare layer order centrally to manage resets, vendor styles, components and utilities predictably.

```css
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;
@layer components {
  .button { padding: .75rem 1rem; }
}
```

calc() combines compatible values with arithmetic; min() and max() choose bounds; clamp(min, preferred, max) creates a fluid value with limits. minmax() defines grid track bounds and fit-content() caps intrinsic sizing. Use spaces around + and - in calc() and ensure units are compatible.

```css
.title { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
.container { width: min(100% - 2rem, 70rem); }
```

Browsers apply default styles such as margins, heading sizes and form-control appearance. A reset removes or standardizes defaults; normalize-style approaches preserve useful defaults while reducing inconsistencies. A common baseline is box-sizing:border-box for all elements and pseudo-elements.

```css
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; }
```

background-image paints one or more images behind an element's content. Control tiling with background-repeat, placement with background-position, scaling with background-size (cover fills/crops; contain fits), and scrolling behavior with background-attachment. Ensure text contrast and provide meaningful content images with HTML img instead.

```css
.hero {
  background-image: url("hero.jpg");
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}
```

Gradients are generated images: linear-gradient() transitions along an axis, radial-gradient() radiates from a center, conic-gradient() rotates around a center, and repeating variants repeat color stops. Gradients can be layered with images and are useful for scalable decoration without image files.

```css
.banner { background: linear-gradient(120deg, #2563eb, #9333ea); }
.orb { background: radial-gradient(circle, white, transparent); }
```

block participates in block flow and normally takes available width; inline flows with text and ignores some box dimensions; inline-block flows inline but accepts box dimensions; none removes the element from layout. flex and grid establish layout contexts; flow-root creates a block formatting context.

```css
.row { display: flex; gap: 1rem; }
.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
```

static is normal flow; relative stays in flow but can be offset; absolute is removed from flow and positioned against its containing block; fixed is generally viewport-relative; sticky behaves like relative until a scroll threshold is reached. Positioning changes layout behavior and stacking.

```css
.card { position: relative; }
.badge { position: absolute; inset: .5rem .5rem auto auto; }
```

flex-grow distributes positive free space, flex-shrink distributes negative free space, and flex-basis establishes the initial main size. The shorthand is commonly flex: grow shrink basis. Understand min-width:auto and set min-width:0 when content must be allowed to shrink.

```css
.layout { display: flex; }
.sidebar { flex: 0 0 16rem; }
.content { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
```

@media applies rules when media conditions match. min-width is mobile-first and applies at or above a breakpoint; max-width applies at or below it. Combine conditions with and, comma-separated alternatives, and range syntax where supported.

```css
@media (min-width: 48rem) {
  .navigation { display: flex; }
}
```

Use max-width:100% and height:auto to prevent images exceeding their containers. object-fit:cover fills a fixed frame with cropping; contain preserves the whole asset. object-position controls the crop focus. Use aspect-ratio to reserve consistent media space.

```css
img { display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
.thumbnail { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; object-fit: cover; }
```

Pseudo-classes represent element states. :hover responds to pointer hover, :active during activation, :focus when focused, :focus-visible when the browser determines a visible focus indicator is needed, and :focus-within when a descendant is focused. Preserve clear keyboard focus.

```css
.button:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
.button:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid orange; outline-offset: 2px; }
```

 :not() excludes matches; :is() groups alternatives and takes the most specific argument; :where() groups alternatives with zero specificity; :has() selects an element based on matching descendants or relative selectors. Use :has() carefully and keep selectors understandable.

```css
.field:has(input:invalid) { border-color: crimson; }
.card :where(h2, p) { margin-block: .5rem; }
```

@keyframes defines named animation states using from/to or percentages. animation properties control name, duration, timing, delay, iteration, direction, fill mode and play state. Keep motion purposeful and avoid flashing or motion that blocks task completion.

```css
@keyframes fade-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}
.panel { animation: fade-in 300ms ease both; }
```

Custom properties use names beginning with -- and are read with var(--name, fallback). They inherit by default and follow the cascade, making them useful for theme tokens and component configuration. Define shared tokens on :root and override them in scoped themes.

```css
:root { --brand: #2563eb; --space: 1rem; }
.button { background: var(--brand); padding: var(--space); }
```

calc() combines compatible values with arithmetic; min() and max() choose bounds; clamp(min, preferred, max) creates a fluid value with limits. minmax() defines grid track bounds and fit-content() caps intrinsic sizing. Use spaces around + and - in calc() and ensure units are compatible.

```css
.title { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
.container { width: min(100% - 2rem, 70rem); }
```

A container query responds to an ancestor container's dimensions or properties rather than the viewport. Establish a query container with container-type (commonly inline-size), optionally name it, then use @container to adapt reusable components to their actual available space.

```css
.card-wrapper { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 32rem) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr; }
}
```

Native CSS nesting places related selectors inside a parent rule, reducing repetition. Use & to explicitly reference the parent selector when needed. Be mindful that nested selector lists can affect specificity; keep nesting shallow and readable.

```css
.card {
  padding: 1rem;
  & h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  &:hover { box-shadow: 0 4px 16px #0002; }
}
```

@supports tests whether a browser supports a CSS declaration or condition. Provide a usable baseline first, then add enhanced rules inside @supports. Feature detection is preferable to assuming support from browser names or user-agent strings.

```css
@supports (display: grid) {
  .layout { display: grid; }
}
```

JavaScript is a high-level, dynamically typed, multi-paradigm programming language used to make applications interactive and build complete software systems. In browsers it can read and update the DOM, respond to user events, validate forms, and communicate with servers. With runtimes such as Node.js, it can also run on servers and build command-line tools.

Core characteristics:
- Single-threaded execution model for ordinary JavaScript code, with asynchronous APIs for non-blocking work.
- First-class functions, objects, closures, and prototype-based inheritance.
- Automatic memory management through garbage collection.
- Standardized language features defined by ECMAScript.

Example:
```js
const button = document.querySelector("button");
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  document.querySelector("p").textContent = "Hello, JavaScript!";
});
```
The browser provides `document` and event APIs; JavaScript supplies the logic. JavaScript is not limited to browsers.

HTML defines a page's structure, CSS controls presentation, and JavaScript adds behavior. In a browser, JavaScript can select HTML elements through the DOM, read or change their content and attributes, update classes, and respond to events.

Example:
```html
<button id="toggle">Toggle message</button>
<p id="message" class="hidden">Welcome!</p>

<script>
  const button = document.querySelector("#toggle");
  const message = document.querySelector("#message");

  button.addEventListener("click", () => {
    message.classList.toggle("hidden");
  });
</script>
```
CSS can define `.hidden { display: none; }`. Prefer changing classes or state rather than repeatedly assigning inline styles. Keep content in HTML, presentation in CSS, and behavior in JavaScript where practical.

Browser Developer Tools help inspect, debug, and profile web applications. Open them through the browser menu or common shortcuts (often F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I). The Console displays logs, warnings, and errors and allows JavaScript to be executed in the current page context.

Useful console methods:
```js
console.log("General information");
console.info("Informational message");
console.warn("Potential issue");
console.error("Failure");
console.table([{ name: "A", score: 10 }, { name: "B", score: 20 }]);
console.group("Details");
console.log("Nested output");
console.groupEnd();
```
Other important panels include Elements (DOM/CSS inspection), Sources (breakpoints and stepping), Network (requests and responses), and Performance (runtime profiling). Treat console output as a debugging aid, not as a substitute for proper error handling or automated tests.

